\title{SigDoTS: significance testing for spatial domains}
\description{
Domain-detection methods divide a tissue section into spatial domains, but the number of
domains comes from a parameter, not from a test. SigDoTS tests which of the proposed domains
are supported by the data. Starting from more candidate domains than you expect (\code{K0} of
them), it arranges them in a tree by spatial adjacency and expression similarity and tests
the splits of the tree from the top down, each against a model in which the part being split
is a single domain, with the spatial correlation of the noise and the correlation between
genes estimated from the data. The parts of the tree that are not split further are the
domains; the probability that any of the kept splits is false is at most \code{alpha}.
}
\section{The functions}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=sigdots]{sigdots()}} runs the test; it takes an expression matrix and coordinates, a Seurat
object or a SpatialExperiment object, and the number of candidate domains \code{K0} (or your own
candidate domains).
\item \code{\link[=sigdots_control]{sigdots_control()}} holds the settings that rarely need changing.
\item \code{\link[=sigdots_prepare]{sigdots_prepare()}} makes the expression matrix from a raw counts matrix.
\item \code{print()} and \code{summary()} (\code{\link[=summary.sigdots]{summary.sigdots()}}) show the split tree and the tests.
\item \code{\link[=domains]{domains()}} gives the domain of every spot as a table; \code{\link[=add_domains]{add_domains()}} writes it into
a Seurat or SpatialExperiment object.
\item \code{plot()} (\code{\link[=plot.sigdots]{plot.sigdots()}}) draws the domains, the candidate domains, the tree and the
spatial correlation.
\item \link{sigdots_example} is a small simulated dataset to try the package on.
}

